                "event": "Flash Flood Warning",
                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Flagstaff AZ",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued October 22 at 2:32AM MST until October 22 at 8:15AM MST by NWS Flagstaff AZ",
                "description": "At 332 AM MDT, a river gauge on Chine Creek within Canyon de Chelly\nNational Monument reported water levels had fallen below flood stage.\nHowever, flooding will remain a threat in areas downstream of Canyon\nde Chelly through the morning hours. Flash flooding is already\noccurring.\n\nHAZARD...Life threatening flash flooding. Thunderstorms producing\nflash flooding.\n\nSOURCE...River gauge reported.\n\nIMPACT...Life threatening flash flooding of Chine Creek.\n\nFor Chinle Creek at Chinle:\nAt 3:00am the stage was 3.4 feet.\nFlood Stage is 4.0 feet.\nImpacts:\nAt 3.6 feet: Chinle Creek becomes impassable to recreational\nvehicles.\nAt 4.0 feet: Water begins to encroach upon structures just upstream\nof the Highway 64 bridge near the Canyon de Chelly welcome center.\nAt 4.3 feet: Flooding to homes and residences in Chinle on left bank\ndownstream from Highway 64 bridge.\n\nSome locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nChinle, Canyon De Chelly National Monument and Mummy Cave Overlook.\n\nThis includes the following recreation areas and campgrounds...\nJunction Overlook, White House Overlook, Antelope House Overlook and\nFace Rock Overlook.\n\nThis includes the following creeks, washes and rivers...\nChinle Wash, Coyote Wash, Muerto, Canyon del, Sheep Dip Creek,\nBlack Rock Canyon and Nazlini Wash.",
                "instruction": "Be especially cautious at night when it is harder to recognize the\ndangers of flooding.\n\nStay away or be swept away! The banks of creeks and washes are\nunstable and unsafe during any flooding event.",
                "response": "Avoid",
